# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# Local data stores. These hold ground observations, farm records and
# calibration points - real people's fields and real people's money. They are
# generated locally and must not travel with the code.
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Photographs submitted through the scouting form. Images of real
# people's fields, taken at recorded coordinates - they stay local.
observations/

# Earth Engine credentials and local environment
.env
.env.*
*credentials*.json
.config/earthengine/

# Snapshot archives of this project
*.zip

# Engine output. Results are reproducible from the code plus geometry; the
# checked-in sample under docs/ is the one deliberate exception.
# Any engine output. The earlier rule was `results*.json`, which anchors at the
# start of the name and therefore missed live_results.json - it was committed and
# pushed before anyone noticed. Match the word anywhere in the name instead.
*results*.json
*_results.json
*_report.json
irrigation_results.json
# The two files under docs/ are deliberate: they are the demo data the
# apps draw when the repo is first cloned, and both carry a note saying
# the measurements are real and the boundaries are invented.
!docs/sample_results.json
!docs/farm_report_demo.json

# Field boundaries drawn in the app. Real polygons are somebody's tenancy and
# their coordinates; the save button writes here by default, so it must not be
# a file that gets committed by a careless `git add -A`.
my_fields.geojson
/*_fields.geojson

# Exported reports. Engine output over real boundaries.
farm_report*.html
*_report.html

# The users file: password hashes and which farms each account may see.
# Never committed. A hash is not a password, but it is a thing to attack
# offline at leisure, and the farm scoping tells an attacker the shape of
# the deployment. It is also per-machine configuration, not source.
users.json
*users*.json

# coverage output
.coverage
htmlcov/
